-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The British composer Samuel Coleridge-Taylor (1875-1912) had a West African father, but in much of his music, the dominant influence is Dvorak rather than the 'Dark Continent' (as Africa was known in the composer's time).  His greatest success came with his settings of Longfellows 'The Song of Hiawatha'.  This short suite of five pieces dates from 1911, and is a reworking for piano of incidental music he wrote for a staged version of Alfred Noyes' 'The Forest of Wild Thyme'.  This is amongst my favourite of his piano works as it is full of variety and caprice, and the piano writing is idiomatic and light-textured.  It is not to be confused with his 1906 work 'Scenes de Ballet'.
